CAT = "oacpSpelling"; SEG = "SPL"
Q = [
("Which is the correct spelling of the word for close, ongoing watch kept on a suspect?", "surveillance", ["surveilance", "surveillence", "surveilliance"], "Surveillance has a double l followed by -ance. Surveillence is a common slip because the ending sounds like -ence."),
("Which is the correct spelling of the word police use for an event they record in a report?", "occurrence", ["occurence", "occurrance", "ocurrance"], "Occurrence doubles both the c and the r and ends in -ence. Occurence, with one r, is the most frequent error."),
("Which is the correct spelling of the word meaning to make room for or provide for?", "accommodate", ["accomodate", "acommodate", "accommadate"], "Accommodate has a double c and a double m. Accomodate, with one m, is the usual mistake."),
("Which is the correct spelling of the word for repeated unwanted conduct that alarms a person?", "harassment", ["harrassment", "harasment", "harassmant"], "Harassment has one r and a double s. Harrassment doubles the r by mistake, likely by analogy with embarrass."),
("Which is the correct spelling of the military and police rank between sergeant and captain?", "lieutenant", ["lieutenent", "leiutenant", "lieutennant"], "Lieutenant begins lieu- and ends -ant. Lieutenent changes the ending to -ent, which is a common error."),
("Which is the correct spelling of the word for a special right or advantage?", "privilege", ["priviledge", "privelege", "privilige"], "Privilege has no d and an i, then e, then e. Priviledge adds a d by analogy with knowledge."),
("Which is the correct spelling of the word meaning to make someone feel awkward or ashamed?", "embarrass", ["embarass", "embaress", "imbarrass"], "Embarrass doubles both the r and the s. Embarass, with one r, is the most common misspelling."),
("Which is the correct spelling of the word for a person who links two agencies or groups?", "liaison", ["liason", "liaision", "laison"], "Liaison has an i on each side of the a: l-i-a-i-s-o-n. Liason drops the second i, which is the usual slip."),
("Which is the correct spelling of the word for the person who commits a crime?", "perpetrator", ["perpertrator", "perpatrator", "perpetraitor"], "Perpetrator is spelled perpe-tra-tor. Perpertrator adds an extra r after the second e."),
("Which is the correct spelling of the word meaning required or needed?", "necessary", ["neccessary", "necesary", "neccesary"], "Necessary has one c and a double s. Neccessary doubles the c, a very common error."),
("Which is the correct spelling of the word for the area over which a police service has legal authority?", "jurisdiction", ["jurisdicton", "juristiction", "jurusdiction"], "Jurisdiction is juris- plus -diction. Juristiction swaps the d for a t, which matches how some people say it but not the spelling."),
("Which is the correct spelling of the word for a failure to take reasonable care?", "negligence", ["negligance", "neglegence", "negligense"], "Negligence ends in -ence and has i after the g. Negligance uses the -ance ending, a common error."),
('A report reads, "Heavy rain did not ___ the search of the ravine." Which word fills the blank?', "affect", ["effect", "affects", "effected"], "Affect is the verb meaning to influence, and it follows did not in its base form. Effect is usually a noun meaning a result, so it does not fit here."),
('A report reads, "The cruiser had a crack across ___ windshield." Which word fills the blank?', "its", ["it's", "its'", "it"], "Its, with no apostrophe, is the possessive form. It's means it is, which would make the sentence read it is windshield."),
('A report reads, "The two witnesses gave ___ statements separately." Which word fills the blank?', "their", ["there", "they're", "theirs"], "Their shows possession, as in the statements belonging to the witnesses. There refers to a place, and they're means they are."),
('A report reads, "The school ___ called police about the break-in." Which word fills the blank?', "principal", ["principle", "principel", "principall"], "Principal is the head of a school. Principle means a rule or belief, so it cannot be the person who called."),
('A report reads, "The sergeant paid the cadet a ___ on her clear notes." Which word fills the blank?', "compliment", ["complement", "complimint", "complements"], "A compliment is a remark of praise. A complement is something that completes or goes well with something else."),
('A report reads, "The accused asked to speak with legal ___ before answering." Which word fills the blank?', "counsel", ["council", "councel", "counsil"], "Counsel means a lawyer or legal advice. A council is an elected or appointed body, such as a city council."),
('A report reads, "Officers may ___ with the search once the warrant arrives." Which word fills the blank?', "proceed", ["precede", "procede", "preceed"], "Proceed means to go forward, and it is spelled with -ceed. Precede means to come before, which does not fit the sentence."),
('A report reads, "The officer used open questions to ___ details from the witness." Which word fills the blank?', "elicit", ["illicit", "ellicit", "illicet"], "Elicit is a verb meaning to draw out. Illicit is an adjective meaning unlawful, so it cannot take an object here."),
('A report reads, "Investigators returned to the ___ of the collision." Which word fills the blank?', "site", ["sight", "cite", "sights"], "A site is a place or location. Sight means vision or something seen, and cite means to quote or refer to."),
('A report reads, "The second suspect was taller ___ the first." Which word fills the blank?', "than", ["then", "that", "as"], "Than is used to compare two things. Then refers to time or order, as in first this, then that."),
('A report reads, "Police located the driver ___ licence was suspended." Which word fills the blank?', "whose", ["who's", "whom", "whos"], "Whose is the possessive form, showing the licence belongs to the driver. Who's is short for who is or who has."),
('A report reads, "The van was ___ when the cyclist struck it." Which word fills the blank?', "stationary", ["stationery", "stationarey", "stationnary"], "Stationary means not moving. Stationery means writing paper and envelopes, which makes no sense for a van."),
('In the sentence "The officer kept the two suspects in seperate rooms," which word is misspelled?', "seperate", ["officer", "suspects", "rooms"], "The correct spelling is separate, with an a after the p. Officer, suspects and rooms are all spelled correctly."),
('In the sentence "The jury acquited the defendant after a lengthy trial," which word is misspelled?', "acquited", ["defendant", "lengthy", "trial"], "The correct spelling is acquitted, with a double t before -ed. Defendant can look odd with its -ant ending, but it is correct."),
('In the sentence "Officers began their persuit at the intersection," which word is misspelled?', "persuit", ["intersection", "officers", "began"], "The correct spelling is pursuit, starting pur-. Intersection is long but correctly spelled."),
('In the sentence "The sargeant reviewed every statement before signing," which word is misspelled?', "sargeant", ["reviewed", "statement", "signing"], "The correct spelling is sergeant, starting ser-. Reviewed, statement and signing are all correct."),
('In the sentence "The witness definately recognized the motorcycle," which word is misspelled?', "definately", ["recognized", "motorcycle", "witness"], "The correct spelling is definitely, built on finite. Recognized is correct; the -ize ending is standard in Canadian spelling."),
('In the sentence "The woman recieved a threatening message overnight," which word is misspelled?', "recieved", ["woman", "threatening", "overnight"], "The correct spelling is received, following i before e except after c. Threatening and overnight are both correct."),
('In the sentence "Police siezed the truck and its contents as evidence," which word is misspelled?', "siezed", ["truck", "contents", "evidence"], "The correct spelling is seized, with e before i, an exception to the usual rule. Truck, contents and evidence are correct."),
('In the sentence "The passenger was unconcious when paramedics arrived," which word is misspelled?', "unconcious", ["paramedics", "passenger", "arrived"], "The correct spelling is unconscious, with sc before the i. Passenger, paramedics and arrived are all spelled correctly."),
]
